body: |-
  ## What We Built

  ### Export Filename
  The ExportDialog previously hardcoded all downloads as `animation.{ext}`. Now it accepts an optional filename parameter, and AtelierStudio exposes a `setFilename()` public API. When the CLI inline app loads a file, it extracts the base name (e.g., `the-brain` from `the-brain.atelier`) and passes it through so downloads match the source file name.

  ### Export All
  New batch export feature accessible from the sidebar footer in the studio CLI. Users pick a format (GIF/MP4/WebM) and click "Export All" — the system iterates every loaded .atelier file, parses it, creates an offscreen canvas + ImageCache, calls `exportDocument()`, and POSTs the binary output to a new `/api/export` endpoint that writes files to disk alongside the source.

  A progress overlay shows current file name and overall progress count. Errors are logged to the browser console for debugging.

  ### H.264 Profile Fix
  MP4 export was using `avc1.42001f` (Baseline Level 3.1) which only supports up to 720p. Updated to try High Profile candidates (Level 5.1 → 4.2 → 4.0) before falling back, matching the multi-candidate pattern already used by WebM export. This fixed the "codec not supported for 1920×1080 at 60fps" error.

  ### CLI Version Fix
  `atelier --version` was hardcoded to `0.1.0`. Changed to read dynamically from package.json via `createRequire`.

  ## Architecture Decisions
  - Export All creates a fresh offscreen canvas and ImageCache per file rather than reusing a shared one, ensuring clean state between exports
  - Binary export uses a separate `/api/export` endpoint (not the existing `/api/file` text endpoint) to handle raw binary data correctly
  - H.264 codec selection follows the same candidate-list pattern as WebM for consistency
